  "knowledgePattern": "Use a units-digit cycle without evaluating a power tower",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "number"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The units digits of successive positive powers of 7 are 7, 9, 3, 1 and then repeat, because multiplication by 7 returns from 1 to 7. Thus the outer power only needs its exponent’s remainder on division by 4.</p><p>The exponent is itself a power of 7 to an odd exponent: every level of the remaining tower is odd. Since 7 leaves remainder −1 modulo 4, every odd power of 7 leaves remainder −1, or 3, modulo 4.</p><p>The exponent therefore selects the third position of the units-digit cycle, which is 3. Answer C. No part of the tower needs to be evaluated numerically.</p>",
